      {
        "id": "p29-q84-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(f(y))=f'(y)\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(f(y))=f'(y)\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$, by the chain rule with $y=y(x)$.",
          "worked": "Since $y=y(x)$, $f(y)$ is a composite function. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{d}{dx}(f(y))&=\\frac{df}{dy}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\\\\\frac{d}{dx}(f(y))&=f'(y)\\frac{dy}{dx}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q84-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By differentiating both sides with respect to $x$, show that for $x^2+y^2=1$, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}$ where $y\\ne0$. Differentiate $y^2$ as a composite function.",
          "worked": "Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0\\\\2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-2x\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{x}{y},\\qquad y\\ne0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
